Q: Using Javascript&#39;s atob to decode base64 doesn&#39;t properly decode utf-8 strings A: <p>Decoding base64 to UTF8 String</p>
<p>Below is current most voted answer by @brandonscript</p>
<pre class="lang-js prettyprint-override"><code>function b64DecodeUnicode(str) {
    // Going backwards: from bytestream, to percent-encoding, to original string.
    return decodeURIComponent(atob(str).split('').map(function(c) {
        return '%' + ('00' + c.charCodeAt(0).toString(16)).slice(-2);
    }).join(''));
}
</code></pre>
<p>Above code can work, but it's very slow. If your input is a very large base64 string, for example 30,000 chars for a base64 html document. It will need lots of computation.</p>
<p>Here is my answer, use built-in TextDecoder, nearly 10x faster than above code for large input.</p>
<pre class="lang-js prettyprint-override"><code>function decodeBase64(base64) {
    const text = atob(base64);
    const length = text.length;
    const bytes = new Uint8Array(length);
    for (let i = 0; i &lt; length; i++) {
        bytes[i] = text.charCodeAt(i);
    }
    const decoder = new TextDecoder(); // default is utf-8
    return decoder.decode(bytes);
}
</code></pre>
<p>From 2026, all browsers support two new methods <code>Uint8Array.fromBase64()</code> and <code>Uint8Array.prototype.toBase64()</code>. The above code can be simplified to:</p>
<pre class="lang-js prettyprint-override"><code>function decodeBase64(base64) {
    return new TextDecoder().decode(Uint8Array.fromBase64(base64));
}

function encodeBase64(str) {
    return new TextEncoder().encode(str).toBase64();
}
</code></pre>
<p>Note: <code>Uint8Array.fromBase64()</code> and <code>Uint8Array.prototype.toBase64()</code> also support an options parameter, that can specify base64 format (&quot;base64&quot; or &quot;base64url&quot;).</p>

Q: Get Date of Previous Monday A: <pre class="lang-cs prettyprint-override"><code>DateTime m = DateTime.Now;
DayOfWeek dow = m.DayOfWeek;

// if m is Sunday then we subtract 6 to get the Monday of this week
// for all other days it is enough to subtract the difference to Monday
DateTime mondayOfCurrentWeek = m.AddDays(dow == DayOfWeek.Sunday ? -6 : -((int)dow - (int)DayOfWeek.Monday))
</code></pre>

Q: Why does this async function sometimes return undefined even though I return a value? A: <p>From now on, add one new-line after every bracket, so your &quot;getData&quot; function should look like this:</p>
<pre><code>async function getData() {

  if (Math.random() &gt; 0.5) {
    return &quot;ok&quot;;
  }

}
</code></pre>
<p>Now that it is easier to see, it is easier to mentalize.</p>
<p>Let's walk through the logic of your code. &quot;If a newly generated random number is greater than 0.5, exit the function and return the string 'ok'.&quot; Another way to verbalize that you wrote is: &quot;if a newly generated random number is greater than 0.5, return 'ok', otherwise exit the function with no return.&quot;</p>
<p>Every if statement is actually &quot;if {condition} do this, otherwise do this.&quot;</p>
<blockquote>
<p>what is the correct way to handle this so the function behaves predictably?</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Write your code by hand, on paper, including all the stupid &quot;just happens&quot; parts, like the &quot;other exit the function with no return.&quot; You must internalize code logic, how your language think, on your own, and this is the fastest way. The point of writing it by hand on paper is to separate that act from typing on the screen.</p>
